WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.700
Protect, inform, sell: the three jobs of your package.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.120
This is the video for Module 1 of Product Packaging and Branding.

3
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:17.220
Packaging is the silent salesperson that stands beside your product when you are not there. It has three jobs, in this order.

4
00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:30.720
First, protect. Keep out air, moisture, dust, light and ants, and survive a hot bus, a dusty market table and a rider's box in the rain. If the package fails to protect, nothing else matters.

5
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:42.910
Second, inform. Tell the buyer what it is, how much is inside, what it is made from, who made it, and when to use it by. Your batch number lets you trace any complaint.

6
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:53.180
Third, sell. Buyers often decide in a few seconds, so the front of your pack must say clearly what the product is and why it is worth the price.

7
00:00:54.430 --> 00:01:05.930
Amaka sells chin chin in thin nylon bags with no label. Customers complain it goes soft in three days, and shops will not stock it because there is no name or date.

8
00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:13.920
She sells about 50 bags a week at 500 naira, and each nylon bag costs her about 20 naira.

9
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:30.900
She switches to food-grade stand-up pouches with a zip, sealed with a small impulse heat sealer. That is about 120 naira per pack. She adds a sticker with her brand, weight, ingredients, phone number, batch number and best-before date.

10
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.860
She raises the price to 700 naira to cover the cost.

11
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:46.060
The chin chin stays crisp, two shops agree to stock it, and her profit per pack goes up. Protection came first, information second, selling power third.

12
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:54.820
These three words are not the same. Your brand is the name, look and promise people remember, and the reputation you earn.

13
00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:10.430
The package is the physical container: the jar, lid, pouch or box. The label carries the printed information and design. The front that faces the buyer is called the principal display panel, or P D P.

14
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.100
Step one. Write 10 possible names. Cross out any that are hard to spell or longer than three words.

15
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.470
Step two. Cross out any close to a well-known brand.

16
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:32.080
Step three. Search the rest online. For example, Funke in Abeokuta dropped Zobo Fresh because several sellers already used it.

17
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:43.680
Step four. Say your top three to five customers and ask which they remember the next day. Funke's customers remembered Ruby Zobo, which says what the product is.

18
00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:50.770
Step five. Check business name availability on the CAC portal before you print anything.

19
00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:58.640
Then keep it simple: a logo that reads small and in one colour, two or three colours, and at most two fonts.

20
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:12.390
Most plastic containers show a number from 1 to 7 in a small triangle. It tells you the plastic type. Number 1, P E T, suits cold drinks like zobo, but not very hot filling.

21
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.120
Number 2, H D P E, is strong and suits soaps and detergents.

22
00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.870
Number 5, P P, suits creams and food tubs, and handles warmer products better.

23
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.540
Glass is an excellent barrier and looks premium, but it is heavy and breaks.

24
00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:41.640
Sealed laminated pouches give strong protection at low cost. The number alone does not prove food safety, so always ask for food-grade containers.

25
00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:50.020
Never reuse a bottle that held chemicals, fuel, insecticide or detergent for food or cosmetics.

26
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:59.970
Pack in a clean, covered area away from animals, with hands washed and hair covered. Wipe the rim before closing, so the seal grips.

27
00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:12.640
Heat sealers and hot fills can burn. Keep fingers away from the sealing bar, unplug the sealer after use, keep it away from children, and do not overload one extension socket.

28
00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:23.610
To remember. Protect, inform, sell, in that order. Know the difference between brand, package and label. Test your name, and choose food-grade containers.

29
00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:31.960
And test samples with your real product for a week before you order in bulk. The best label cannot rescue the wrong container.

30
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:39.680
Now audit one product you sell, draft your one-page brand kit, and run a seven-day container trial.

31
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.970
Continue with the lessons below.
